  "notes": "あんよ is baby talk ({幼児語|ようじご}) for \"foot\" or \"walking.\" It comes from {足|あし} (foot/leg) softened into a child-friendly form.\n\nUSAGE:\nFunctions as a noun (foot/leg) and a する verb (to walk).\n- あんよが{上手|じょうず}: good at walking (praise for toddlers)\n- あんよする/あんよできる: to walk (baby talk)\n- よちよちあんよ: toddling walk\n\nMILESTONE:\nOften used when discussing a baby's first steps — a key developmental milestone. Parents eagerly await when their child can あんよする.\n\nRELATED BABY TALK WORDS:\n- おてて: hands (from {手|て})\n- おめめ: eyes (from {目|め})\n- ねんね: sleep (from {寝|ね}る)\n- まんま: food (from ごはん/まま)\n- おくち: mouth (from {口|くち})\n\nREGISTER:\nUsed only with babies and very small children. Using it with adults would be humorous or condescending.",
